// Recognition section for professionals — the pain points, in order of how
// often practitioners say them out loud: insight that doesn't move anything
// (named mechanism: the block is held in the body/nervous system, not the
// story), the session clock, the pre-verbal ceiling, the moment real emotion
// arrives (overwhelm/shutdown), and what stays in the practitioner's own
// body. The accent quote is Jacob's own (copy book §2); the pull line is
// copy book B.12.
//
// The audience is international — many read English as a second language.
// Keep the body copy in plain international English: complete sentences,
// common words, no unglossed idioms.
---

<section class="we-opening">
  <div class="we-opening-inner">
    <div class="we-eyebrow">You know this moment</div>
    <h2 class="we-opening-head">
      Your clients understand their block,<br />
      and yet <em>nothing moves.</em>
    </h2>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      If you work with people — in a practice, a classroom, a session room, a
      team — you know the moment. The pattern has a name now. The story has
      been told twice. And a spoken story is always edited: in words, people
      show you only what they have decided to show. The insight is correct,
      and still nothing changes — because the block does not live where the
      insight lives. It is held in the body, in the nervous system, deeper
      than explanations can reach. The session ends at the same point as the
      last one.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      You know the other versions too. A mixed feeling needs ten minutes to
      explain, because words can only come one at a time — and the clock is
      running. The earliest experiences, stored in the body before a child
      can speak, cannot be reached through conversation at all: anyone who
      works with attachment or the first years of life knows this limit. And
      then the moment nobody talks about at conferences: real emotion finally
      arrives in the room — the person in front of you becomes overwhelmed,
      or shuts down — and the honest question inside you is
      <em>what do I do now?</em>
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      Then there is the quieter version — the one about you. All week, you
      sit with other people in their most difficult moments, your own nervous
      system present for every one of those hours. Some of what they bring
      stays in your body after they have gone. Most professionals have no
      practice of their own where any of that is ever heard.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      This masterclass is about exactly those moments. In Somatic Vocal
      Healing the question is not <em>tell me more about it</em> — the
      question is <em>what is the sound of it?</em> Sound before story: a
      feeling that would need many careful sentences arrives complete, in one
      honest tone. And the person in front of you was born knowing how to
      make that tone.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      It doesn't replace what you do. It works underneath it. Talking is
      top-down: it starts from the story, in the thinking mind. Sounding is
      bottom-up: it starts from the body — at the exact level where the block
      is held.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-accent">
      "I've never had a therapist ask me: <em>could you make a sound for
      that?</em> It's odd and sad that it doesn't happen — because it's still
      so natural to us." — Jacob
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-pull">
      Talking circles it.<br />Sounding enters it.
    </p>
    <a href="#register" class="we-link-cta">
      Reserve your seat <i class="ph-light ph-arrow-right"></i>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
